.page httpd 1 a tiny web server for static files

# SYNOPSIS
*httpd* [*-p* _PORT_] [*-d* _DIR_] [*-b* _ADDRESS_] [*-1*] [*-q*]

# DESCRIPTION
`httpd` serves the files below a directory (the current one unless `-d` says otherwise) over HTTP/1.1 on a TCP port (8080 by default; ports below 1024 need root). It answers `GET` and `HEAD`, sets `Content-Type` from the file name extension, sends `Content-Length` and closes the connection after each answer. A directory is answered with its `index.html` when there is one and with a generated listing otherwise; a directory named without its trailing slash gets a redirect. Names containing `..` are refused, so nothing outside the served directory can be reached, and missing files get a 404.

Every connection is handled by a child process, so a slow client does not hold up the others. Each request is logged on standard error (`ADDRESS "GET /path" STATUS`) unless `-q` is given. It runs in the foreground; put it in the background with `&` and stop it with `kill`.

It is meant for demos and for moving files between machines: there is no authentication, no TLS and no access control beyond the file permissions of the user who started it.

# OPTIONS
@ *-p* _PORT_
    The port to listen on.
@ *-d* _DIR_
    The directory to serve.
@ *-b* _ADDRESS_
    Listen on one address only (default: all of them).
@ *-1*
    Answer a single request and exit.
@ *-q*
    Do not log requests.

# EXAMPLES
    httpd -p 8080 -d /HumanVillage/reimu/www &
    fetch http://127.0.0.1:8080/index.html

# NOTES
Under QEMU's user-mode networking the guest is not reachable from the host unless a port is forwarded: start QEMU with `hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:8080-:8080` on the `-netdev user` option and the host can browse to `http://127.0.0.1:8080/`.

# SEE ALSO
fetch(1), nc(1), network(7)
